# 📘 VOLUME 2: OPERATING SYSTEM INTERVIEW HANDBOOK (25 QUESTIONS)

# 🎯 Q1. What is an Operating System?

## What interviewer is testing

Do you understand why an OS exists?

## Answer

An Operating System acts as a bridge between:

```text
User ↔ Hardware
```

It manages:

* CPU
* Memory
* Files
* Devices

---

## Real World Example

Imagine a restaurant.

```text
Customer = User

Kitchen = Hardware

Manager = Operating System
```

Customer doesn't directly talk to the chef.

Manager coordinates everything.

---

# 🎯 Q2. Why Do We Need an OS?

## Answer

Without OS:

```text
Program must manage
CPU
Memory
Disk
Keyboard
Printer
```

With OS:

```text
Program asks OS
OS handles resources
```

---

# 🎯 Q3. What is a Process?

🔥 Most Asked

## Answer

A process is a program currently executing.

Example:

```text
chrome.exe
```

When running:

```text
Chrome Process
```

---

## Real World Example

```text
Recipe Book = Program

Cooking = Process
```

A recipe sitting on a shelf isn't doing anything.

Cooking means execution.

---

# 🎯 Q4. Process vs Program

| Program        | Process           |
| -------------- | ----------------- |
| Static         | Dynamic           |
| Stored on Disk | Running in Memory |
| Passive        | Active            |

---

### Interview Trick

```text
Program = Blueprint

Process = Construction Work
```

---

# 🎯 Q5. What is a Thread?

🔥 Asked Everywhere

## Answer

A thread is the smallest unit of execution within a process.

---

## Chrome Example

Chrome Process contains:

```text
Tab 1 Thread

Tab 2 Thread

Download Thread

Render Thread
```

---

## Easy Memory Trick

```text
Process = House

Threads = Family Members
```

They share resources.

---

# 🎯 Q6. Process vs Thread

| Process       | Thread        |
| ------------- | ------------- |
| Heavyweight   | Lightweight   |
| Own Memory    | Shared Memory |
| Slow Creation | Fast Creation |
| Secure        | Less Secure   |

---

### FAANG Follow-up

Why are threads faster?

Answer:

Because threads share memory.

No separate address space creation.

---

# 🎯 Q7. What is Multithreading?

## Answer

Running multiple threads within one process.

---

## Example

YouTube App

```text
Video Playing

Comments Loading

Recommendations Loading
```

Simultaneously.

---

# 🎯 Q8. What is Context Switching?

🔥 Product Companies Love This

## Answer

Saving one process state and loading another.

---

## Real World Example

Teacher teaching:

```text
Student A

Student B

Student C
```

Teacher switches between students.

That switch is Context Switching.

---

### Drawback

```text
Context Switching Cost
```

CPU spends time switching.

---

# 🎯 Q9. What is CPU Scheduling?

## Answer

OS decides:

```text
Which process gets CPU next?
```

---

## Real Example

Movie ticket counter.

Only one person served at a time.

Need a scheduling strategy.

---

# 🎯 Q10. FCFS Scheduling

First Come First Serve.

---

## Example

Queue:

```text
P1

P2

P3
```

Execution:

```text
P1 → P2 → P3
```

---

### Drawback

Long process blocks everyone.

---

# 🎯 Q11. Round Robin Scheduling

🔥 Common Interview Question

Each process gets fixed time slice.

---

Example:

```text
Quantum = 2
```

Execution:

```text
P1 → P2 → P3 → P1
```

---

## Real World

Teacher gives every student 2 minutes.

Fair scheduling.

---

# 🎯 Q12. What is Starvation?

## Answer

A process waits forever.

---

## Real Example

VIP queue.

Normal people never get chance.

---

# 🎯 Q13. What is Deadlock?

🔥 Super Important

## Answer

Two or more processes wait forever for each other.

---

## Real Example

Pooja has:

```text
Pen
```

Rahul has:

```text
Notebook
```

Pooja waits for notebook.

Rahul waits for pen.

Nobody moves.

Deadlock.

---

# 🎯 Q14. Four Conditions of Deadlock

Remember:

```text
MEHW
```

### M

Mutual Exclusion

### E

Hold and Wait

### H

No Preemption

### W

Circular Wait

---

# 🎯 Q15. How to Prevent Deadlock?

Break one condition.

Example:

```text
No Circular Wait
```

Deadlock disappears.

---

# 🎯 Q16. What is a Semaphore?

🔥 FAANG Favorite

## Answer

Semaphore controls access to shared resources.

---

## Example

Parking Lot:

```text
5 Parking Slots
```

Semaphore value:

```text
5
```

When cars enter:

```text
5 → 4 → 3 → 2
```

Controls access.

---

# 🎯 Q17. Mutex vs Semaphore

| Mutex            | Semaphore          |
| ---------------- | ------------------ |
| One Resource     | Multiple Resources |
| Binary           | Counting           |
| Ownership Exists | No Ownership       |

---

## Easy Trick

```text
Mutex = One Key

Semaphore = Many Tickets
```

---

# 🎯 Q18. What is Race Condition?

🔥 Frequently Asked

## Answer

Multiple threads modify shared data simultaneously.

Result becomes unpredictable.

---

## Example

Balance:

```text
1000
```

Thread A:

```text
Withdraw 500
```

Thread B:

```text
Withdraw 500
```

Both read:

```text
1000
```

Wrong result possible.

---

# 🎯 Q19. What is Critical Section?

## Answer

Part of code accessing shared resources.

---

Example:

```cpp
balance = balance - 500;
```

Must be protected.

---

# 🎯 Q20. What is Paging?

🔥 Memory Management Favorite

## Answer

Memory divided into fixed-size pages.

---

## Real Example

Large book.

Instead of loading:

```text
Entire Book
```

Load:

```text
Page 1
Page 2
Page 3
```

as needed.

---

# 🎯 Q21. What is Virtual Memory?

🔥 Top Product Company Question

## Answer

Using disk as an extension of RAM.

---

## Example

RAM:

```text
8 GB
```

Program needs:

```text
12 GB
```

OS temporarily uses disk.

---

### Benefit

Programs larger than RAM can run.

---

# 🎯 Q22. What is Fragmentation?

## Internal Fragmentation

Unused memory inside allocated block.

---

## External Fragmentation

Free memory exists but scattered.

---

## Real Example

Bookshelf with many tiny gaps.

Enough space exists.

But not continuous.

---

# 🎯 Q23. What is Thrashing?

🔥 Advanced Question

## Answer

CPU spends more time swapping pages than executing.

---

## Example

Student constantly opening:

```text
Book A

Book B

Book C

Book D
```

instead of studying.

---

# 🎯 Q24. User Mode vs Kernel Mode

## User Mode

Limited access.

Example:

```text
Chrome
VS Code
Spotify
```

---

## Kernel Mode

Full hardware access.

Operating System runs here.

---

# 🎯 Q25. What Happens When You Open Chrome?

🔥 FAANG-Level Integration Question

Expected Flow:

```text
Double Click Chrome

OS Loads Program

Process Created

Memory Allocated

Threads Created

CPU Scheduled

Chrome Starts Running
```

